1. Actuator allocation vs. distance-to-riser — the figure you're missing
Aggregate across all 15 trials: x-axis is distance to the riser (−0.8 m to +0.4 m), thrust on the left axis, wheel speed on the right, mean with a ±1σ band, vertical line at contact.

If the policy ramps thrust before contact and decays it after, that curve is the entire paper in one plot — and crucially it's continuous, with no step change anywhere. That's your mode-free claim made visual rather than asserted. A mode-switched controller would show a discontinuity at a threshold; yours won't.

Everything needed is already in your trial CSVs (pos_x/y, action_3, wheel_meas_*) plus the step position from make_step_map.py. It costs you nothing extra to collect.

2. Fig. 1: composite + actuator strip
Already designed. Result and claim in one image, and it answers R4's "the video shows a robot that can't balance" directly.

3. Energy–success Pareto front
One point per w_E ∈ {0, 0.25, 1, 2, 4}, success rate against energy per traverse, operating point marked. This is what justifies your title. If there's a knee, it's a great figure; if the front is flat, it's still a publishable finding and you report it honestly.

4. Actuator identification, 4 panels — your most distinctive asset

(a) thrust: bench points + degree-4 fit, RMSE 3.9e-3 N
(b) wheel:  commanded vs achieved, with the 23.6 rad/s ceiling and the
            saturated region shaded — this is the figure that explains
            why wheel_scale 0.5 fixed hardware
(c) servo:  sim 2.0 vs measured 10.0 rad/s, with tau = 119 ms marked
(d) latency budget: sense -> policy -> actuator, against tau
Almost every RL paper has a domain-randomisation table. Almost none shows measured actuator mismatches and their consequences. Panel (b) alone tells a story nobody else is telling, and (c) is the honest limitation that makes reviewers trust the rest.

5. Hardware trials: per-trial strip + Clopper–Pearson CIs
Policy vs decoupled baseline, N=15 each. Answers C1 and C2 in one figure. Show individual trials as dots, not just bars — R3 asked for statistics, and 15 visible points is more convincing than an error bar.

6. Reward-hacking comparison
Success rate over training under XY-only vs your four criteria, same policy. The gap between the curves is the inflation, and it turns a methodological claim into a measurement. Cheap — it's a re-scoring of data you already have.

